{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And I have come down to rescue him and to bring him up, etc.</b> The reason for His saying <b>and I have come down</b> is that, according to the measure of the greatness of El Elyon, it is not beneath His dignity to perform everything that would bring His words to a base man like Pharaoh and to say things to him in His name — and how much more so when he reaches the level of denial, to the point of saying (Exodus 5:2) ״מִי ה׳״, heaven forbid. Go out and learn from what our Sages expounded in Eichah Rabbah in the Proem (Lamentations Rabbah, Proem 14) on the verse (Proverbs 29:9) ״אִישׁ חָכָם נִשְׁפָּט אֶת אִישׁ אֱוִיל״ — it does not say ״שָׁפַט״ (judged) but rather ״נִשְׁפָּט״ (was judged), etc. — examine their words there. Therefore HaShem said <b>and I have come down</b> — He consented to a diminishing of His honor, as it were, for the sake of rescuing Israel, as He said: ״to rescue him.״ And the intent of this rescue is that He would rescue him from now, from the affliction and from the servitude, and that is His saying <b>from the hand of Egypt</b>, who enslave them." "And His saying <b>and to bring him up from the land</b> — meaning, when the time of redemption arrives, from that point until twelve months, I will bring them up from the land, etc., so that they would not remain there any longer even without affliction. And the reason He did not bring them up immediately and at once is that the time of redemption that HaShem had fixed had not yet arrived. And with this my mind is at rest regarding one inquiry: why did HaShem extend twelve months over the plagues of Egypt — and our Sages, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 9:12), some of them said that each plague lasted three weeks and the warning one week, and some said the reverse — and why would HaShem delay the redemption? He should have brought the plagues one after another and redeemed them immediately, and a single day or a single hour of warning would have sufficed, according to the law of warning for Israel, which requires no set time, and all the more so for the nations of the world, who require no warning at all. Also, why was one warning not sufficient for all the plagues? Rather, it is because the time had not yet arrived — HaShem was clothing that time to satiate them with bitterness in the interim, until the time arrived and He redeemed them immediately without any delay. And if you say: what difference would it have made if HaShem had redeemed them before this time? Further, why did HaShem bring them out hastily at the end, at a time when if they had delayed a little they would have been trapped, so that they needed to hurry and could not tarry — should He not have brought them out at a leisurely time?" "<b>Behold</b> — in light of what has preceded us, that the essence of the exile is to refine the sparks that were absorbed in the fifty gates of impurity, as I have noted this matter several times (Genesis 49:9) — with this the mind will rest regarding both questions. For if He had brought them out before this, they would have lost the refinement of that portion. And know that the people of HaShem attained, in their collective aspect included within Moses — who was described by the name of His people — the attainment of forty-nine gates of understanding. And the reason they did not attain the fiftieth gate is that attainment depends only on the exertion of the one who attains; and since Israel did not enter into the fifty gates of impurity to refine it, they did not attain the dimension of holiness corresponding to it. And we have been promised that in the time to come, El Elyon will bestow upon us the Torah of life that is in the fiftieth gate, and its attainment comes through the agency of the exiles — and in particular through the final exile we attain the matter." "And the reason that Israel was at risk in Egypt in the refinement of the fiftieth gate is that they were not people of Torah — whereas the later generations, through the agency of their Torah, will attain to enter the fiftieth gate and extract its swallowing from its mouth, and then the dimension of impurity will be utterly consumed. From this it follows that whenever HaShem would bring Israel out before any given time, they would diminish the refinement and would be diminished in their attainment. Therefore He brought them out at the final point of the forty-nine and before they would enter the fiftieth gate — and this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: He redeemed them immediately (Mishnah Pesachim 10:5, per the reading of the Rambam)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {1 "זִבּוּלָא בַּתְרַיְתָא rendered literally as 'hastily at the end'; the Aramaic idiom refers to the hasty exodus at midnight."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:04.879803502Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}